  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 226 PM CDT Sat Aug 29 2026\n\nWater vapor imagery and RAP analysis this afternoon depict broad \nridging over the Central and Southern Plains. At the surface, a \nsurface trough extends from far western Kansas northeast into the \nNorthern Plains. A tightened surface pressure gradient ahead of this \nboundary has promoted breezy south winds so far today, and these are \nforecast to continue overnight through Sunday alongside dry \nconditions. Similar to today, high temperatures to close out the \nweekend will climb into the 100-105 degree range. A few showers and \nstorms developing across the High Plains Sunday evening may find \ntheir way into portions of central KS late Sunday night into early \nMonday morning, though these should remain isolated and low-impact.\n\nHot, breezy, and mostly dry conditions are expected to continue \nacross the forecast area through the upcoming week with ridging \noverhead. Chances for rain will likely reside well to our west \nthrough at least mid-week behind the aforementioned surface trough \nstalled across western KS. Hot daytime high temperatures will be \nlikely as well with values in the 100-104 degree range through at \nleast Wednesday and perhaps beyond. Toward the end of the week, long-\nrange models hint at isolated rain chances shifting east into \ncentral KS as the ridge translates toward the eastern CONUS, though \nconfidence in these remains low this far out. As such, meaningful \nrainfall in the forecast area appears unlikely through the upcoming \nweek.",
